      </div>
      <div class="card p-4 text-center bg-canvas">
        <p class="text-xs uppercase text-mute tracking-wider">To Find Grams</p>
        <p class="mt-1 font-semibold text-ink text-lg">Protein Calories ÷ 4</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-center text-brand font-medium mt-2"><a href="/protein-calculator/">Need your daily target? Use our free Protein Calculator &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <p>If you're meticulously tracking your diet, one of the most common questions you'll ask is: <strong>how many calories are in a gram of protein?</strong> Understanding the relationship between protein grams and total calories is the foundation of nutrition, whether you're using a <a href="/macro-calculator/">macro calculator</a> to build muscle or a <a href="/weight-loss-calculator/">weight loss calculator</a> to shed fat.</p>

  <p>In this comprehensive guide, we will break down the exact math behind <strong>protein to calories</strong>, provide rapid conversion charts for common protein targets, and explain the fascinating science of how your body actually burns calories just to digest protein.</p>

  <ArticleImage src="/assets/images/articles/protein-structure.svg" alt="Illustration showing that 1 gram of protein equals 4 calories." priority={true} />

  <h2>Why is Protein Exactly 4 Calories? (The Atwater System)</h2>
  <p>In the late 19th century, chemist Wilbur Olin Atwater burned different macronutrients in a calorimeter to measure the exact amount of heat (energy) they released. He established the baseline measurements we still use today:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 mt-4 mb-8">
    <li><strong>Protein:</strong> 4 calories per gram</li>
    <li><strong>Carbohydrates:</strong> 4 calories per gram</li>
    <li><strong>Fats:</strong> 9 calories per gram</li>
  </ul>
  
  <p>This means protein and carbs are equally calorie-dense, while fats carry more than double the energy. This is why high-protein diets are so effective for weight loss—you can eat a massive physical volume of food for very few calories.</p>

  <ExpertTake quote="When my clients switch from a high-fat diet to a high-protein diet, they are always shocked by how much food they get to eat. Because protein is only 4 calories per gram, 200 calories of chicken breast takes up significantly more space in your stomach than 200 calories of olive oil, keeping you full for hours." />

