  <h2>What is a Good Protein to Calorie Ratio? (The 10-to-1 Rule)</h2>
  <p>When you are shopping at the grocery store, you might wonder how to spot a genuinely "high protein" food versus a clever marketing gimmick. This brings up a highly searched question: <strong>what is a good ratio of protein to calories?</strong></p>
  <p>Fitness experts and nutritionists often recommend the <strong>10 to 1 calorie to protein ratio</strong>. Here is how it works:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 mt-4 mb-8">
    <li>Drop the last zero from the total calories of the food item.</li>
    <li>The remaining number is the <em>minimum</em> amount of protein (in grams) the food should have to be considered a highly efficient protein source.</li>
    <li><strong>Example:</strong> If a cup of Greek yogurt has 120 calories, drop the zero (12). Does it have at least 12 grams of protein? If it has 15g, it surpasses the 10-to-1 rule. It is a fantastic protein source!</li>
    <li><strong>Example:</strong> A protein bar has 250 calories. It should have at least 25g of protein. If it only has 10g, it is essentially a candy bar, heavily weighted toward carbs and fats.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to Calculate Protein from Total Calories (Percentages)</h2>
  <p>Many diet plans (like the Zone Diet or standard bodybuilding splits) prescribe macros as percentages, such as "Eat 30% protein." Here is how to convert that percentage back into grams using the 4 kcal/g rule.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Example: You are eating 2,000 calories a day and want 30% to come from protein.</strong></p>
  <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 mt-4 mb-8">
    <li>Find 30% of 2,000 calories: <strong>(2,000 x 0.30) = 600 protein calories</strong></li>
    <li>Divide those calories by 4 to get grams: <strong>600 ÷ 4 = 150 grams of protein</strong></li>
  </ol>
  <p>You can skip this manual math entirely by using our <a href="/macro-calculator/">Macro Calculator</a>, which does percentage-to-gram conversions automatically.</p>

  <h2>The "Thermic Effect" Hidden Calorie Burn</h2>
  <p>While a gram of protein technically contains 4 calories, your body actually burns a massive amount of energy just trying to digest it. This is called the <strong>Thermic Effect of Food (TEF)</strong>.</p>
  
  <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 mt-4 mb-8">
    <li><strong>Fat TEF:</strong> 0-3% of calories burned during digestion</li>
    <li><strong>Carb TEF:</strong> 5-10% of calories burned during digestion</li>
    <li><strong>Protein TEF:</strong> 20-30% of calories burned during digestion</li>
  </ul>
  
  <p>This means if you eat 100 calories of pure protein (25 grams), your body burns about 25 of those calories simply breaking down the amino acid chains. The net yield to your body is closer to 75 calories! This hidden "metabolic advantage" is why high-protein diets are overwhelmingly recommended by nutritionists for fat loss.</p>

